menu "app_epd -- the ePaper panel"

config APP_EPD_ENABLE
    bool "Drive a UC8159 ePaper panel"
    default y
    help
        The Pimoroni Inky Impression 5.7", 600x448, seven colours, UC8159 controller.
        Switch it off to build the firmware without a panel attached.

comment "Pins -- defaults are the Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3 (PLAN 3b)"

config APP_EPD_SCLK_GPIO
    int "SCLK"
    default 8
    help
        XIAO D8. Also an ESP32-C3 strapping pin, which must be high at reset. SCLK is an
        output here and the panel's SCLK is high impedance, so the line should float --
        but if boot becomes intermittent, a 10k pull-up to 3v3 on this pin is the first
        thing to try.

config APP_EPD_MOSI_GPIO
    int "MOSI"
    default 10

config APP_EPD_CS_GPIO
    int "Chip select"
    default 3

config APP_EPD_DC_GPIO
    int "Data/command"
    default 4

config APP_EPD_RST_GPIO
    int "Reset"
    default 5

config APP_EPD_BUSY_GPIO
    int "Busy"
    default 6
    help
        Driven by the panel, low while it is working.

        Must NOT be GPIO9 or GPIO2 on an ESP32-C3: both are strapping pins, and GPIO9 low
        at reset puts the chip into download mode. The panel holds BUSY low exactly while
        the board is coming up, so that combination gives a board which boots only
        sometimes with nothing on the console to explain it.

if APP_EPD_ENABLE

config APP_EPD_SPI_HZ
    int "SPI clock (Hz)"
    default 10000000
    help
        Pimoroni's driver uses 3 MHz on this panel and Soldered's uses 10 MHz on the same
        controller. 3 MHz was the conservative start; 10 MHz is what this board actually
        runs, measured rather than assumed.

        Measured on the bench board with a dozen jumper wires, which is the worst case a
        ribbon will ever be: the 134 400-byte transfer takes 375 ms at 3 MHz and 118 ms at
        10 MHz, and both refresh correctly. PLAN 3c predicted 360 and 110.

        It is worth keeping in proportion. The saving is about a quarter of a second
        against a refresh that takes 26 seconds of physics, so this is not why the panel
        is slow and never will be. Drop back to 3000000 if a longer or untidier ribbon
        starts producing corrupt frames -- the symptom would be a picture with the right
        colours in the wrong places, not a blank panel.

endif

endmenu
